WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.870
Hello, and welcome to our second drink of Hot Girl.

2
00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:08.310
We're doing something a little differently this week.

3
00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:16.690
When we revisited our breakdown of Hot Girl, we noticed that there were quite a few things that we missed that we could have talked about.

4
00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.690
And we kind of wanted to break it down again.

5
00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:26.330
Yes, because so many of you have also written to us and asked us if we would break down some of the Peacock superfan episodes.

6
00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.750
So we thought, why not do a full breakdown of the superfan episode of Hot Girl?

7
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.090
So this Wednesday, we're going to do it.

8
00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:36.370
We're going to be breaking down the superfan episode of Hot Girl.

9
00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.290
And if you don't have Peacock, don't worry, we'll fill you in.

10
00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.310
There is still a lot to enjoy.

11
00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:45.090
For example, I got to do my Dunder Mifflin hallway investigation.

12
00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.170
You're very excited about it.

13
00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:54.430
Well, Dave Rogers told us there's a deleted scene from Hot Girl that really sheds some light on where all these hallways go.

14
00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:55.690
I can't wait.

15
00:00:55.690 --> 00:01:00.990
I also really want to talk about this extended scene between Michael and Ryan where they're talking about music.

16
00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:01.710
It's so funny.

17
00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:02.390
It's hilarious.

18
00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:03.350
All right.

19
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:07.070
Please enjoy this second drink of Hot Girl, and we will see you Wednesday.

20
00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.040
I'm Jenna Fisher.

21
00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:13.360
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

22
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.640
We were on The Office together.

23
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:15.880
And we're best friends.

24
00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:19.700
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

25
00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:26.680
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

26
00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:27.840
We're The Office ladies.

27
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.550
Ladies, that's us.

28
00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:32.290
Hey there.

29
00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:33.270
We are here.

30
00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:36.670
We are here, and we're excited because today we're talking about Hot Girl.

31
00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:42.630
Hot Girl, Season 1, Episode 6, written by Mindy Kaling and directed by Amy Heckerling.

32
00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:44.810
I was really excited to meet Amy Heckerling.

33
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.430
You know, growing up, guys, I might be a child of the 70s.

34
00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:51.710
No judgment, the oldie sitting here.

35
00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:55.370
No, and she directed Fast Times at Ridgemont High and Clueless.

36
00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:56.090
Clueless.

37
00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:56.630
Clueless.

38
00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:57.430
So I was excited.

39
00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.150
I remember there was a bit of a buzz.

40
00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:00.590
Yeah, there was a little bit of a buzz.

41
00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:01.090
That she was coming in.

42
00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:01.930
We were very excited.

43
00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:03.570
I didn't know how to make small talk with her.

44
00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:04.110
And guess what?

45
00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:04.750
I failed.

46
00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.110
All right, Jenna, hit us with a synopsis.

47
00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.050
All right, synopsis of this episode.

48
00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:17.090
Michael allows a woman selling purses to set up in the conference room for the day, and Michael and Dwight compete for her attention.

49
00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:18.570
Very simple.

50
00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:19.510
Very simple.

51
00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:20.010
And yet.

52
00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.630
So loaded for Pam.

53
00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.150
This was really a big Pam episode.

54
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:25.350
It really was.

55
00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.370
So should we start with some fast facts, then?

56
00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:28.490
I know you want to.

57
00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:29.010
You know.

58
00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:33.250
My fast fact, number one, this is the first episode written by Mindy Kaling.

59
00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:38.510
Yes, Mindy Kaling's so smart, so funny, and she was one of our most prolific writers on The Office.

60
00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.570
She wrote a total of, Jenna, ready?

61
00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:41.110
What?

62
00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:42.630
22 episodes.

63
00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:43.390
Wow.

64
00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:44.130
I know.

65
00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.110
That's like she wrote an entire season of The Office.

66
00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:47.470
I know.

67
00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.970
Of our nine, because the season's usually about 22, 24 episodes.

68
00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:52.990
I have a fan question about Mindy.

69
00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:53.510
All right.

70
00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:54.370
This is from Bear.

71
00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:55.170
Hi, Bear.

72
00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:55.610
Yes.

73
00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.750
Bear asked, this episode was written by Mindy Kaling.

74
00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:01.930
What was it like to work with her as a writer versus an actor?

75
00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:02.770
Oh, Bear.

76
00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:03.530
Good question.

77
00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:04.130
I know.

78
00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:05.230
You know what?

79
00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:11.330
I loved when Mindy was the writer of the episode, because the writer of the episode on our show

80
00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:15.990
would stay on set all week and produce their episode with the director.

81
00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.450
So they kind of worked very side by side with the director.

82
00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:21.110
Yes, and they were sort of the liaison back to the writer's room.

83
00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:22.510
I love that our show did this.

84
00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:23.670
Not all shows do that.

85
00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:27.590
And I really think you benefit from having the writer of the episode on the set with

86
00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:27.750
you.

87
00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:31.090
Well, Mindy, it was so easy to make Mindy laugh.

88
00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:34.930
Like, she was so ready to love everything you were doing.

89
00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:37.890
You were filled with such confidence when Mindy was on the set.

90
00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:42.210
And she also was really good on the spot coming up with new jokes, because that was another

91
00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:45.050
thing that the writer of the episode would do.

92
00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:48.190
They would pitch you improvisations on the spot.

93
00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:52.530
Yeah, and Mindy, for me, as someone with like an improv background, I loved being able to

94
00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.050
pitch to Mindy because she's so smart.

95
00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:57.110
So I could like say, hey, Mindy, could I, you know, try this?

96
00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.390
And then she would just instantly be like, oh, that'd be great.

97
00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:00.270
Or how about you do this?

98
00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:03.350
Like, she was such a wonderful, creative collaborator.

99
00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:07.170
I always felt like Mindy was such a big advocate for the character of Pam as well.

100
00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.650
I think she was in the writer's room pitching storylines for Pam.

101
00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.850
And this was a very Pam-centered episode, as we said.

102
00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:15.530
Pam has feelings.

103
00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:16.810
She has a lot of feelings.

104
00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:18.430
Pam has a lot of feelings.

105
00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:19.130
She does.

106
00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:21.530
This leads me into my second fastback.

107
00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:26.610
I was texting with Mindy about this episode in preparation, and she told me something.

108
00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:27.230
What?

109
00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.250
The original title of this episode was Purse Girl.

110
00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:31.050
Oh!

111
00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:32.730
Do you kind of remember that?

112
00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:37.090
I actually do, because I feel like I have referred to this as Purse Girl a few times,

113
00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:37.930
because it's in my brain.

114
00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:38.230
Yeah.

115
00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:42.170
She also told me that her original draft of this was 39 pages.

116
00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:43.390
Wow.

117
00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:48.350
Which is a lot, because usually the number of pages that a script is, that's how many

118
00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:49.690
minutes of screen time it is.

119
00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:55.070
So her script, if we did it exactly as written, would have been 39 minutes, which we would

120
00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:55.950
not have been able to air.

121
00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:59.690
I really wish we could have done that, and I wish we could have filmed all 40 pages of

122
00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:00.810
that instead of 22.

123
00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:01.730
22 minutes.

124
00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:06.150
I remember they used to have those very long table drafts, and then they would cut a bunch

125
00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:08.810
of stuff out, and they would make something they called the candy bag.

126
00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:09.090
Yeah.

127
00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:13.690
And those were all of the cut scenes, or they were additional takes on scenes.

128
00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:19.670
So whatever talking head wound up in the script, there would be five different takes on that

129
00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:21.170
talking head in the candy bag.

130
00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:24.870
And so we would sit down to do our talking heads, and they would say, all right, well,

131
00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.130
we're going to do the scripted one, but then we're going to do three of them from the

132
00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:28.610
candy bag.

133
00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:33.410
Oh, I remember going to do a talking head, and they handed me three pages of alts, like

134
00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:34.090
right in the minute.

135
00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:37.590
I'm like, oh, right, okay, I'm going to have to have a photographic memory.

136
00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:39.130
You have to really be on your toes.

137
00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:40.390
I think that's something that...

138
00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:41.890
It kept us on our toes, for sure.

139
00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:42.870
I loved it.

140
00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:43.710
I loved it, too.

141
00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:47.730
And then they used to let me play around and sort of put my spin on it, too, which was

142
00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:48.190
always fun.

143
00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:49.890
All right, Fisher, what else you got?

144
00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:52.730
All right, I think it's time to talk about Amy AdaMs.

145
00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:55.790
Okay, I have a lot of note cards here in front of me.

146
00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:59.550
I see, and a lot of them have the words Amy or Amy Adams on them.

147
00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.350
Okay, you tell me when you're ready, because I've got a note card ready to go.

148
00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:04.290
Well, this is our third fast fact.

149
00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:04.670
Okay.

150
00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:07.130
Amy Adams was the purse girl.

151
00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:10.750
Mindy told me that Amy was her favorite from the very beginning.

152
00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:14.370
This is who she wanted to cast as the purse girl from her audition.

153
00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:19.350
But something that I think people don't realize is that Amy Adams wasn't Amy Adams when she

154
00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:20.510
was cast as the purse girl.

155
00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:22.990
She became Amy Adams later.

156
00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:25.770
And that's an interesting story, actually, because we...

157
00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:31.450
We filmed this episode in the fall of 2004, but they didn't start airing until the spring

158
00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:32.490
of 2005.

159
00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:37.970
And that fall, she was nominated for an Academy Award for her role in Junebug.

160
00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:38.290
Right.

161
00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:41.950
When she did this episode of The Office, no one had seen Junebug yet, because Junebug

162
00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:43.970
came out in August of 2005.

163
00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:44.370
Yes.

164
00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:48.670
And I remember her first day on set, we were talking, and I was like, how are things

165
00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:48.950
going?

166
00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:50.210
She's like, well, you know, it's good.

167
00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:55.070
I mean, I was on a show on CBS called Dr. Vegas, but they let me go.

168
00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:56.450
Oh, that's right.

169
00:06:56.690 --> 00:06:58.070
She was like, I kind of got fired.

170
00:06:58.130 --> 00:06:58.830
And I was like, no.

171
00:06:59.030 --> 00:06:59.590
And she was like, yeah.

172
00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:00.650
And I was really down.

173
00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:01.890
And I was bummed out.

174
00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:05.050
But then, you know, I did this little indie movie called Junebug.

175
00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:07.070
And I feel so good about it.

176
00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:08.550
And it's going to come out later in the year.

177
00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:10.030
And I was like, oh, that sounds great.

178
00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:10.630
Yeah.

179
00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:12.330
Let me build you up, Amy AdaMs.

180
00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:14.470
Amy, you're going to be just fine.

181
00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:16.370
And she was.

182
00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:16.930
And she was.

183
00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:19.350
But Amy is one of the nicest people.

184
00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:21.430
She's so, so sweet.

185
00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:22.910
Oh, I loved Amy.

186
00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:25.070
It was so fun working with her.

187
00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:30.510
There were a lot of questions from fans asking if we planned to bring this character back

188
00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:31.530
after we shot it.

189
00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:33.470
Well, we didn't think we were going to have a show.

190
00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:33.950
Right.

191
00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:36.010
There were no real plans to bring her back.

192
00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:39.390
But then when the show got picked up, they wanted to bring her back.

193
00:07:39.390 --> 00:07:42.470
But now she was Academy Award nominated Amy AdaMs.

194
00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:46.130
I was like, well, I hope we can get her back.

195
00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:46.670
And we did.

196
00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:48.110
She came back for two more episodes.

197
00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:48.690
Yes.

198
00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:53.110
And she was such a great foil, too, for Pam and Jim and all of that.

199
00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:54.530
I loved watching that play out.

200
00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:58.990
Not only was Amy Adams super nice on set and just like such a pleasure to work with.

201
00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:02.710
But I don't know, probably like two years later, I ran into her.

202
00:08:02.890 --> 00:08:04.730
I had a friend visiting from Alabama.

203
00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:07.550
And you guys, I don't ever go out.

204
00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:08.910
So my friend was coming in town.

205
00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:11.110
And I was like, oh, I'm going to take her somewhere fancy.

206
00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:13.970
That hotel, Chateau Marmont, that's fancy.

207
00:08:14.090 --> 00:08:14.690
We'll go there.

208
00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:15.790
Because I just had it in my head.

209
00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:17.450
Yeah, like the Hollywood fancy place to eat.

210
00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:18.110
It was a fancy place.

211
00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:18.370
Yeah.

212
00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:19.990
I mean, it's like half an hour from my house.

213
00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:21.170
It was such a hall.

214
00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:23.370
But I was like, no, I'll take my friend from Alabama there.

215
00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:24.350
So we went.

216
00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:26.010
And we were having dinner.

217
00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:28.190
And Amy Adams was at a table.

218
00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:32.110
And she sent over two glasses of champagne to my friend and I.

219
00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:34.450
And I was like, they were like, oh, this is so classy.

220
00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:35.670
Classy move, Amy AdaMs.

221
00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:37.250
And I was like, who's this from?

222
00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:38.950
They were like, it's from Amy AdaMs.

223
00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:40.110
And she waved.

224
00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:41.390
And then she came over and we chatted.

225
00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:43.170
Amy Adams is just a class act.

226
00:08:43.310 --> 00:08:47.510
I have a funny Amy Adams story about how people think that we look alike.

227
00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:51.190
So she and I were both at this work party thing.

228
00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:52.450
I can't remember exactly.

229
00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:55.470
I think it was like honoring directors in the industry.

230
00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:56.570
And we were both there.

231
00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.290
And my friend came up to me.

232
00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:01.030
And he said, I am so humiliated right now.

233
00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:02.590
And I said, what happened?

234
00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:09.110
And he goes, I just went up and bear hugged Amy Adams and said, Jenna, you look beautiful.

235
00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:09.910
Oh, no.

236
00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:11.270
Because he mixed us up.

237
00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:14.410
And people have this thing where they think she and I look a lot alike.

238
00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:17.130
We really don't look anything alike in person.

239
00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:20.410
Although I guess I just told a story about someone who mixed us up in person.

240
00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:21.970
I mean, there is a resemblance.

241
00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:25.430
She's perfectly cast because there is a resemblance.

242
00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:27.050
And you're both like beautiful ladies.

243
00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:27.750
Thanks.

244
00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:29.770
But I see a resemblance.

245
00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:31.070
I feel like we could play sisters.

246
00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:31.970
You could.

247
00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:33.010
All right, Hollywood.

248
00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:34.230
You heard it here firSt.

249
00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:36.930
That's how I, that's, I guess that's how I talk when I'm talking to Hollywood.

250
00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:37.890
I guess it is.

251
00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:39.190
Hollywood beware.

252
00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:40.030
Hollywood.

